\section{Discussion}

The permutation calibration results fundamentally change the interpretation of temporal DIF in LLM benchmarks. The raw 31.3\% headline on MMLU is inflated by $\sim$40\% due to temporal cohort structure. After calibration, the genuine instability estimate of $\sim$17\% for MMLU and 7--17\% across six benchmarks is still meaningful (all exceed random controls by 10--40$\times$) but far less dramatic than the uncalibrated figure.

The semi-synthetic framework provides a reusable validation methodology. The cross-subject matching insight (that within-subject matching contaminates the conditioning variable) generalizes to any multi-domain benchmark. However, $\Delta$TPR = 0.254 at $p = 0.5$ suggests that detection power is limited for moderate contamination levels; practical utility requires injection rates $\geq 0.7$ for reliable detection ($\Delta$TPR = 0.484).

The regression analysis reveals that DIF preferentially flags high-discrimination items (OR = 1.58). This is consistent with the psychometric interpretation that items with steeper difficulty curves are more sensitive to group differences, but also raises the concern that DIF may flag the most informative items. The Simpson's paradox (domain improvement effect vanishes after conditioning on item properties) confirms that DIF operates at the item level rather than reflecting domain-wide capability changes.

\section{Limitations}

Three limitations constrain the conclusions of this study. First, MH-DIF is a unidimensional method applied to a 57-subject multidimensional benchmark. The bifactor IRT pilot (\S\ref{sec:bifactor}) failed to converge on 34 of 57 subjects, and converged subjects showed poor fit (RMSEA = 0.089, CFI = 0.74). While MH conditions on observed total score (a nonparametric procedure that does not require IRT model fit), the multidimensionality of MMLU means that total-score stratification may not fully control for latent ability dimensions. Propensity-score matching or MIMIC models could address this in future work.

Second, the ability confound is partially but not fully resolved. Nearest-neighbor matching (plan\_027) reduces the ability gap to $d \approx -0.01$ and C\% from 31.3\% to 28.9\%, explaining only 2.4 pp. However, the 57-dimensional latent structure of MMLU means that matching on total score may leave 6--12 pp of residual confounding from unmatched latent dimensions.

Third, the GSM1K negative correlation ($r = -0.478$, N = 33) is consistent with the interpretation that DIF captures ability improvement rather than item instability. The wide confidence interval ([$-0.71$, $-0.16$]) and small sample size preclude strong conclusions, but this finding challenges the diagnostic interpretation of DIF.

\section{Conclusion}

Naive application of MH-DIF to temporal model cohorts inflates instability estimates by approximately 40\%. Permutation calibration reduces the apparent 31\% instability on MMLU to $\sim$17\%, and across six benchmarks the calibrated range is 7--17\%. The semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching provides a reusable methodology for evaluating any item-level contamination detector, with controlled FPR inflation ($<$ 2\%). Future DIF studies on LLM benchmarks should adopt permutation calibration as standard practice and explore multidimensional DIF methods to address the structural limitations of MH-DIF on multi-subject benchmarks.

