\section{ConStat Perturbation} \label{sec:constat}

ConStat-style perturbation testing on 240 paraphrased MMLU items across 50 models yields a detection rate of 23\% on non-RL models and 7\% on RL models. The ConStat-DIF overlap is minimal (Jaccard = 0.11), indicating orthogonal detection dimensions. Paraphrase semantic similarity averages 0.91, but any detection may partly reflect paraphrase artifacts rather than genuine memorization signals. FDR-controlled FPR = 0.08. This experiment consumed 72 GPU hours.

\section{DVD Generation Variance} \label{sec:dvd}

DVD generation variance analysis (12 models $\times$ 500 items $\times$ 50 responses per item under temperature sampling) produces weak results. Bimodal detection rate = 14\%, verbatim detection power = 0.09, variant detection power = 0.31, FPR = 0.12. DIF-DVD overlap approaches zero (Jaccard = 0.06). The 65 GPU hours invested produced a near-null result; DVD and DIF detect entirely different item populations.

\section{Ensemble Fusion} \label{sec:ensemble}

The planned DIF + ConStat + DVD ensemble was not executed. Upstream signal quality was insufficient for meaningful fusion: pairwise Jaccard values below 0.12 indicate the three methods detect different phenomena rather than the same signal with different noise profiles. Projected 2-of-3 majority voting TPR = 0.15 with FPR = 0.06, reflecting the low inter-method agreement.

\section{Bifactor IRT Equating} \label{sec:bifactor}

A bifactor IRT model for MMLU's 57 subjects failed to converge on 34 of 57 subjects. Items per subject range from 18 (formal logic) to 1,534 (miscellaneous), creating severe sample-size imbalance. Among the 23 converged subjects, fit was poor (RMSEA = 0.089, CFI = 0.74, TLI = 0.71). No reliable anchor items could be selected, and the equating pipeline was terminated. This failure reflects a structural mismatch between bifactor parameterization and MMLU's heterogeneous domain structure, not a software or convergence issue.

\section{Item Generation Calibration} \label{sec:itemgen}

LLM-generated replacement items for DIF-flagged questions (N = 300) show limited calibration accuracy. Prompt-based difficulty targeting achieves $\rho = 0.18$ with empirical difficulty, improving to 0.62 under student simulation and 0.48 in empirical holdout validation ($\rho = 0.72$ on holdout model panel). Subject match rate = 83\%, cognitive level match = 61\%, quality pass rate = 41\% (123 of 300 items usable). The 180 GPU hours consumed produced insufficient quality for the regeneration pipeline originally envisioned.

\section{Cumulative Bayesian DIF} \label{sec:bayesian}

Simulated cumulative Bayesian DIF priors across four administrations show power increasing from 0.38 (single administration, $\delta = 1.0$) to 0.67 (four administrations), falling short of the 0.70 target. At $\delta = 1.4$, three administrations suffice (power = 0.74). FPR remains controlled at 0.055. Prior specification sensitivity is moderate ($\pm 0.08$). Table~\ref{tab:bayesian} reports power by administration count.

