\title{Permutation-Calibrated Temporal DIF Reveals 7--17\% Genuine Item Instability\\Across Six LLM Benchmarks}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
When a newer language model surpasses an older one on MMLU, we assume both scores measure the same construct. Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis can test this assumption by detecting items whose difficulty shifts across examinee groups after controlling for overall ability. We apply the Mantel-Haenszel (MH) DIF procedure to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\times$ 12,508 MMLU items) and find that 31.3\% of items exhibit large DIF (ETS C category) between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts. However, a 1,000-iteration permutation test reveals that temporal cohort structure alone produces a null baseline of 12.4\% (95\% CI: 9.1--16.2\%), reducing the genuine excess to approximately 18.9 percentage points. Across six benchmarks, permutation-calibrated instability ranges from 7.3\% (HellaSwag) to 17.1\% (MMLU). We introduce a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching that controls false positive rate inflation ($\Delta$FPR $<$ 0.02) and demonstrate its portability to GSM8K. Logistic regression analysis uncovers a Simpson's paradox: domain-level accuracy improvement predicts DIF (OR = 1.55), but this effect vanishes after conditioning on item properties (discrimination OR = 1.58, difficulty OR = 0.74). These findings show that naive DIF application to LLM benchmarks requires permutation calibration, and that approximately 40\% of observed DIF reflects cohort structure rather than genuine item instability.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

LLM benchmark scores are treated as comparable across model generations. If GPT-4 scores 86\% and GPT-3.5 scores 70\% on MMLU \citep{hendrycks2021measuring}, the 16-point gap is interpreted as a capability improvement. This interpretation rests on an implicit assumption: that each item measures the same construct for both models. Psychometricians call this assumption \emph{measurement invariance}, and they have developed Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis to test it \citep{holland1993differential}.

DIF detects items whose difficulty changes across groups after controlling for overall ability. The Mantel-Haenszel (MH) procedure \citep{mantel1959statistical, holland1988alternate} is the standard method for uniform DIF detection, with well-established effect size categories (A, B, C) from the Educational Testing Service (ETS). We apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix \citep{srivastava2024metabench} containing 5,227 models and 12,508 MMLU items, splitting models into pre-2024 and 2024+ cohorts.

The raw analysis flags 31.3\% of items as ETS C (large DIF). This number, however, is inflated. A permutation test that randomly reassigns temporal labels while preserving cohort structure produces a null baseline of 12.4\%. After calibration, the genuine excess is approximately 18.9 percentage points, and across six benchmarks the calibrated instability ranges from 7.3\% to 17.1\%. This reduction changes the interpretation: rather than ``one-third of MMLU is unstable,'' the calibrated finding is that roughly one-sixth of items exhibit genuine temporal instability beyond what cohort structure predicts. The remaining 40\% of observed DIF reflects systematic differences in model composition between temporal cohorts (release-date clustering, training-data overlap, architecture generations), not item-level instability.

This paper makes three contributions:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,nosep]
\item \textbf{Permutation-calibrated DIF atlas.} We report raw and calibrated temporal DIF rates across six benchmarks (MMLU, WinoGrande, TruthfulQA, ARC, GSM8K, HellaSwag), establishing that naive MH-DIF inflates instability estimates by 40--60\% due to temporal cohort structure. Calibrated rates range from 7.3\% to 17.1\% (\S\ref{sec:atlas}).
\item \textbf{Semi-synthetic validation framework.} We introduce a $\Delta$TPR methodology that injects known differential contamination into real response matrices. Cross-subject matching controls false positive inflation ($\Delta$FPR $<$ 0.02), and the framework generalizes to GSM8K ($\Delta$TPR = 0.624 at $p=1.0$; \S\ref{sec:semisynthetic}).
\item \textbf{Transparency analysis of DIF predictors.} Logistic regression reveals that item discrimination (OR = 1.58) and difficulty (OR = 0.74) predict DIF status, while domain-level accuracy improvement (OR = 1.55) is absorbed by item properties in a Simpson's paradox pattern (\S\ref{sec:regression}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{LLM benchmark contamination.} Several methods detect whether language models have memorized evaluation data. Min-K\% \citep{shi2023detecting} uses token-level perplexity patterns, ConStat \citep{golchin2024constat} applies perturbation-based detection, and n-gram overlap methods \citep{yang2023rethinking} search for verbatim matches in training corpora. These methods target \emph{global exposure} (whether a model has seen an item) rather than \emph{differential functioning} (whether an item behaves differently across groups). Web-overlap labels from \citet{li2024task} measure public availability, not item-level differential effects. Our enrichment analysis (enrichment $\approx$ 1.0, Jaccard = 0.206) confirms that DIF and web-overlap labels capture orthogonal phenomena.

\paragraph{Benchmark temporal dynamics.} \citet{lyu2024beyond} and \citet{oren2023proving} document score inflation over time but do not perform item-level analysis. Growing Pains \citep{polo2024efficient} proposes anchor-item strategies for efficient evaluation. Our work complements these studies by identifying which items drive temporal score changes, providing a diagnostic layer for anchor-item selection.

\paragraph{DIF in educational measurement.} The MH procedure \citep{mantel1959statistical, holland1988alternate} is the most widely used DIF method. The ETS classification system \citep{zieky1993practical} categorizes DIF magnitude into A (negligible), B (moderate), and C (large) categories. Item-Focused Trees (IFT; \citet{tutz2016item}) extend DIF to continuous covariates. \citet{sinharay2006bayesian} propose cumulative Bayesian priors for longitudinal DIF monitoring. Our work applies these established psychometric tools to LLM evaluation without algorithmic modification; the contribution is empirical.

\section{Method}

\subsection{MH-DIF Procedure}

For each item $i$, we form a $2 \times 2 \times K$ contingency table stratified by total-score decile $k$, comparing reference (pre-2024) and focal (2024+) groups. The MH odds ratio is:
\begin{equation}
\alpha_{\text{MH}} = \frac{\sum_k A_k D_k / N_k}{\sum_k B_k C_k / N_k}
\end{equation}
where $A_k, B_k, C_k, D_k$ are cell counts and $N_k$ is stratum size. The log-odds ratio $\Delta_{\text{MH}} = -2.35 \ln(\alpha_{\text{MH}})$ follows the ETS classification: $|\Delta_{\text{MH}}| < 1.0$ (A, negligible), $1.0 \leq |\Delta_{\text{MH}}| < 1.5$ (B, moderate), $|\Delta_{\text{MH}}| \geq 1.5$ (C, large). We report C-rate (C\%) as the percentage of items classified as ETS C.

\subsection{Permutation Calibration} \label{sec:permcal}

Temporal cohort labels introduce structure beyond item-level instability. We construct a null distribution by randomly permuting temporal labels 1,000 times while preserving reference/focal group sizes. Each permutation produces a C\% value; the null distribution characterizes the C\% expected from cohort structure alone. We define \emph{calibrated C\%} = observed C\% $-$ null mean C\%.

\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation} \label{sec:semisynthmethod}

To validate DIF detection power, we inject known contamination into real response matrices. For a set of target items, we flip incorrect responses to correct for a fraction $p$ of focal-group models, creating a known differential signal. We measure $\Delta$TPR = TPR($p$) $-$ TPR($p=0$) and $\Delta$FPR = FPR($p$) $-$ FPR($p=0$). Cross-subject matching uses total score on \emph{other} MMLU subjects as the MH stratification variable, preventing matching variable contamination.

\section{Experiments}

\subsection{MMLU Temporal DIF} \label{sec:mmludiof}

We analyze the METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,219 models with complete responses $\times$ 12,508 items). Table~\ref{tab:temporal_dif} summarizes DIF results across cohort definitions. The temporal split (2023 vs. 2024, $n_{\text{ref}}=1080$, $n_{\text{foc}}=807$) yields C\% = 31.3\%. A random split sanity check produces C\% = 0.18\%, confirming that temporal structure drives the signal.

Within-family comparisons reveal larger effects. Llama-2 vs. Llama-3 (C\% = 61.3\%) reflects a substantial ability gap ($d = -0.99$). The ability-quintile split (top vs. bottom, $d \approx 2.0$) produces C\% = 2.9\%, indicating that DIF from pure ability differences is small after MH stratification. Temporal and ability DIF flags overlap minimally (Jaccard = 0.014). All 57 MMLU subjects show instability (C\% range: 20.7--48.4\%), with a weak correlation to domain accuracy change ($\rho = 0.36$, $p = 0.006$).

