\subsection{Permutation Null Calibration} \label{sec:permnull}

Table~\ref{tab:permutation_null} reports the permutation null distribution. The null mean C\% = 12.4\% (SD = 1.8\%, 95\% CI: 9.1--16.2\%) indicates that temporal cohort structure alone generates substantial DIF. The observed C\% = 31.3\% exceeds the null ($z = 10.5$, $p < 0.001$), but 39.6\% of the raw signal is attributable to cohort structure. Decomposition of the null identifies three components: release-date clustering ($\sim$7 pp), training-data overlap ($\sim$3 pp), and residual cohort structure ($\sim$2.4 pp). The permuted cohorts show negligible ability gaps (mean $d = 0.001$, SD = 0.052), confirming that the null reflects structural rather than ability confounds.

The calibrated genuine excess is 18.9 pp. This is statistically significant but substantially lower than the uncalibrated 31.3\%. All interpretive claims in subsequent sections use calibrated values.

