\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation} \label{sec:semisynthetic}

We inject contamination into 150 items within a 500-item MMLU subset and measure DIF detection power at injection rates $p \in \{0, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\}$. Table~\ref{tab:semisynthetic} reports detection power. At $p = 0$ (no injection), the baseline TPR = 0.326 and FPR = 0.318, reflecting the high ambient DIF rate. Detection power increases monotonically: $\Delta$TPR = 0.094 at $p = 0.3$, rising to 0.624 at $p = 1.0$. The FPR remains stable across injection levels ($\Delta$FPR = 0.012--0.018 with cross-subject matching).

Table~\ref{tab:matching_fpr} compares cross-subject and within-subject matching strategies. Within-subject matching suffers from matching variable contamination, producing $\Delta$FPR = 0.098 at $p = 0.5$ and 0.196 at $p = 1.0$. Cross-subject matching controls this inflation ($\Delta$FPR = 0.012 at $p = 0.5$, 0.018 at $p = 1.0$), though the residual is not exactly zero due to residual cross-subject correlations.

