\subsection{Cross-Benchmark: GSM8K} \label{sec:gsm8k}

GSM8K replication (1,319 items $\times$ 6,702 models) confirms framework portability. Table~\ref{tab:gsm8k} reports key metrics. Raw C\% = 18.9\% reduces to 9.7\% after permutation calibration (null = 9.2\%). The semi-synthetic validation achieves $\Delta$TPR = 0.818 at $p = 1.0$ (standard matching) and 0.517 (external matching), both exceeding the MMLU values.

A GSM1K ground-truth correlation analysis (N = 33 models with paired GSM8K/GSM1K scores) reveals a negative Pearson $r = -0.478$ ($p = 0.005$, 95\% CI: [$-0.71$, $-0.16$]). Models with larger DIF effects tend to show smaller GSM8K-vs-GSM1K gaps. This correlation admits two interpretations: (1) DIF flags items where newer models genuinely improved, not where contamination occurred; (2) contamination and genuine improvement are confounded at this sample size. With N = 33 and a CI extending to $-0.16$, this finding is exploratory.

