\subsection{Odds Ratio Regression} \label{sec:regression}

We fit four nested logistic regression models to predict item-level DIF status (C vs. non-C). Table~\ref{tab:regression} reports model comparisons. Model 1 (domain-level accuracy change only) yields OR = 1.55 per 0.10 change (CI: [1.19, 2.03]), but McFadden $R^2 = 0.0007$, indicating near-zero explanatory power. Model 2 adds item properties (discrimination, difficulty) and jumps to $R^2 = 0.0523$, with discrimination OR = 1.58 and difficulty OR = 0.74.

A Simpson's paradox emerges: Model 3 (item-level accuracy change) shows a marginally significant OR = 1.12 (CI: [1.02, 1.23]), but in Model 4 (item change + item properties), the accuracy change effect vanishes. Item properties absorb the accuracy change signal entirely ($\chi^2 = 702.1$ for Model 3 vs. Model 4). The full model explains 9.2\% of variance (Nagelkerke $R^2$), leaving 90.8\% unexplained.

