\subsection{Item-Focused Trees} \label{sec:ift}

Item-Focused Trees \citep{tutz2016item} treat release date as a continuous covariate rather than a binary temporal split. Table~\ref{tab:ift} compares IFT with MH. IFT detects C\% = 27.4\% (vs. MH 31.3\%), with 78\% concordance. IFT reveals that 47\% of flagged items exhibit gradual temporal drift rather than abrupt shifts, suggesting that the binary cohort split is a simplification. Computation time increases 8$\times$ (8.2 vs. 1.0 hours) with no power advantage (power ratio = 0.92).

