\subsection{Graceful Degradation} \label{sec:degradation}

We measure validity metrics under progressive item replacement (5\%--50\% of items replaced with LLM-generated alternatives). Table~\ref{tab:degradation} reports rank correlation ($\rho$), reliability ($\alpha$), and construct coverage. Degradation is non-linear: replacing up to 20\% of items has minimal impact ($\rho = 0.987$, $\alpha = 0.912$), but acceleration begins at $\sim$30\%, and at 50\% replacement, reliability drops to 0.88 and coverage to 0.79. A theoretical bound is violated at 42\% replacement. Since permutation-calibrated DIF flags $\sim$17\% of MMLU items, replacement of flagged items alone falls within the safe zone.

