20 & 0.987 & 0.912 & 0.91 \\
50 & 0.962 & 0.880 & 0.79 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Discussion}

The permutation calibration results fundamentally change the interpretation of temporal DIF in LLM benchmarks. The raw 31.3\% headline on MMLU is inflated by $\sim$40\% due to temporal cohort structure. After calibration, the genuine instability estimate of $\sim$17\% for MMLU and 7--17\% across six benchmarks is still meaningful (all exceed random controls by 10--40$\times$) but far less dramatic than the uncalibrated figure.

The semi-synthetic framework provides a reusable validation methodology. The cross-subject matching insight (that within-subject matching contaminates the conditioning variable) generalizes to any multi-domain benchmark. However, $\Delta$TPR = 0.254 at $p = 0.5$ suggests that detection power is limited for moderate contamination levels; practical utility requires injection rates $\geq 0.7$ for reliable detection ($\Delta$TPR = 0.484).

The regression analysis reveals that DIF preferentially flags high-discrimination items (OR = 1.58). This is consistent with the psychometric interpretation that items with steeper difficulty curves are more sensitive to group differences, but also raises the concern that DIF may flag the most informative items. The Simpson's paradox (domain improvement effect vanishes after conditioning on item properties) confirms that DIF operates at the item level rather than reflecting domain-wide capability changes.

\section{Limitations}

Three limitations constrain the conclusions of this study. First, MH-DIF is a unidimensional method applied to a 57-subject multidimensional benchmark. The bifactor IRT pilot (\S\ref{sec:bifactor}) failed to converge on 34 of 57 subjects, and converged subjects showed poor fit (RMSEA = 0.089, CFI = 0.74). While MH conditions on observed total score (a nonparametric procedure that does not require IRT model fit), the multidimensionality of MMLU means that total-score stratification may not fully control for latent ability dimensions. Propensity-score matching or MIMIC models could address this in future work.

Second, the ability confound is partially but not fully resolved. Nearest-neighbor matching (plan\_027) reduces the ability gap to $d \approx -0.01$ and C\% from 31.3\% to 28.9\%, explaining only 2.4 pp. However, the 57-dimensional latent structure of MMLU means that matching on total score may leave 6--12 pp of residual confounding from unmatched latent dimensions.

Third, the GSM1K negative correlation ($r = -0.478$, N = 33) is consistent with the interpretation that DIF captures ability improvement rather than item instability. The wide confidence interval ([$-0.71$, $-0.16$]) and small sample size preclude strong conclusions, but this finding challenges the diagnostic interpretation of DIF.

\section{Conclusion}

Naive application of MH-DIF to temporal model cohorts inflates instability estimates by approximately 40\%. Permutation calibration reduces the apparent 31\% instability on MMLU to $\sim$17\%, and across six benchmarks the calibrated range is 7--17\%. The semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching provides a reusable methodology for evaluating any item-level contamination detector, with controlled FPR inflation ($<$ 2\%). Future DIF studies on LLM benchmarks should adopt permutation calibration as standard practice and explore multidimensional DIF methods to address the structural limitations of MH-DIF on multi-subject benchmarks.

\appendix

\section{RL Robustness} \label{sec:rl}

We test DIF stability when 13 reasoning-RL models are mixed into the focal cohort (Table~\ref{tab:rl}). The 1.6\% mixing ratio (13/820) produces negligible change in C\% (30.5\% vs. 31.3\% baseline), with high DIF flag retention (Jaccard = 0.82, 95\% CI: [0.74, 0.89]). However, this experiment is fundamentally underpowered: 13 models mixed into 820 cannot meaningfully test whether RL training disrupts DIF patterns. ConStat and MinK\% AUROCs on RL models (0.58 and 0.54) are near chance, but N = 13 makes confidence intervals uninformative. TOST equivalence $p = 0.18$ is non-significant, preventing equivalence claims.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{DIF retention under cohort composition variation. Jaccard measured against the full temporal baseline.}
\label{tab:rl}
\begin{tabular}{lrrr}
\toprule
Condition & $n_\text{foc}$ & C\% & Jaccard \\
\midrule
SFT-only focal & 412 & 30.8 & 0.87 \\
Alignment-RL & 477 & 31.1 & 0.89 \\
Reasoning-RL mixed & 820 & 30.5 & 0.82 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{ConStat Perturbation} \label{sec:constat}

ConStat-style perturbation testing on 240 paraphrased MMLU items across 50 models yields a detection rate of 23\% on non-RL models and 7\% on RL models. The ConStat-DIF overlap is minimal (Jaccard = 0.11), indicating orthogonal detection dimensions. Paraphrase semantic similarity averages 0.91, but any detection may partly reflect paraphrase artifacts rather than genuine memorization signals. FDR-controlled FPR = 0.08. This experiment consumed 72 GPU hours.

\section{DVD Generation Variance} \label{sec:dvd}

DVD generation variance analysis (12 models $\times$ 500 items $\times$ 50 responses per item under temperature sampling) produces weak results. Bimodal detection rate = 14\%, verbatim detection power = 0.09, variant detection power = 0.31, FPR = 0.12. DIF-DVD overlap approaches zero (Jaccard = 0.06). The 65 GPU hours invested produced a near-null result; DVD and DIF detect entirely different item populations.

\section{Ensemble Fusion} \label{sec:ensemble}

The planned DIF + ConStat + DVD ensemble was not executed. Upstream signal quality was insufficient for meaningful fusion: pairwise Jaccard values below 0.12 indicate the three methods detect different phenomena rather than the same signal with different noise profiles. Projected 2-of-3 majority voting TPR = 0.15 with FPR = 0.06, reflecting the low inter-method agreement.

\section{Bifactor IRT Equating} \label{sec:bifactor}

A bifactor IRT model for MMLU's 57 subjects failed to converge on 34 of 57 subjects. Items per subject range from 18 (formal logic) to 1,534 (miscellaneous), creating severe sample-size imbalance. Among the 23 converged subjects, fit was poor (RMSEA = 0.089, CFI = 0.74, TLI = 0.71). No reliable anchor items could be selected, and the equating pipeline was terminated. This failure reflects a structural mismatch between bifactor parameterization and MMLU's heterogeneous domain structure, not a software or convergence issue.

\section{Item Generation Calibration} \label{sec:itemgen}

LLM-generated replacement items for DIF-flagged questions (N = 300) show limited calibration accuracy. Prompt-based difficulty targeting achieves $\rho = 0.18$ with empirical difficulty, improving to 0.62 under student simulation and 0.48 in empirical holdout validation ($\rho = 0.72$ on holdout model panel). Subject match rate = 83\%, cognitive level match = 61\%, quality pass rate = 41\% (123 of 300 items usable). The 180 GPU hours consumed produced insufficient quality for the regeneration pipeline originally envisioned.

\section{Cumulative Bayesian DIF} \label{sec:bayesian}

Simulated cumulative Bayesian DIF priors across four administrations show power increasing from 0.38 (single administration, $\delta = 1.0$) to 0.67 (four administrations), falling short of the 0.70 target. At $\delta = 1.4$, three administrations suffice (power = 0.74). FPR remains controlled at 0.055. Prior specification sensitivity is moderate ($\pm 0.08$). Table~\ref{tab:bayesian} reports power by administration count.

